WEBVTT


00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:05.850
With non-patterning knitting machines
and anytime you want to work a hand

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:10.200
manipulated stitch pattern, there's
usually a lot of counting of needles

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:10.920
involved.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:17.940
For simple stitch transfers, you can use
a pencil or a water soluble pen and simply

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.080
Mark the needle bed.

00:00:20.025 --> 00:00:24.735
You could also use your needle
pushers to select sets of needles.

00:00:26.055 --> 00:00:31.485
I've found that cheat sheets come in handy
for more complicated stitch patterns.

00:00:32.505 --> 00:00:36.435
We've created a set of sheets for
the various gauges of machines.

00:00:37.455 --> 00:00:40.995
The grids are sized according
to the needle pitch.

00:00:41.325 --> 00:00:44.355
That's the space between
the centers of the needles.

00:00:45.435 --> 00:00:45.915
Here.

00:00:45.915 --> 00:00:52.935
I'm using a 6.5 millimeter gauge and the
needles of my LK-150 fit exactly in the

00:00:52.935 --> 00:00:54.225
boxes of the grid.

00:00:56.535 --> 00:00:59.655
I've marked my stitch
pattern sequence in pink.

00:01:00.735 --> 00:01:07.755
I'll have two needles that will be small
cables over three rows, and there will be

00:01:07.755 --> 00:01:12.255
six needles in between that are
going to remain in plain knitting.

00:01:14.535 --> 00:01:19.935
As I'm knitting, I just simply line up the
appropriate row on the cheat sheet with my

00:01:19.935 --> 00:01:20.505
needle bed.

00:01:21.535 --> 00:01:23.845
Pull out the needles
according to the chart.

00:01:25.164 --> 00:01:29.545
I moved the cheat sheet along the bed
for the width of my knitting and without

00:01:29.545 --> 00:01:33.865
having to count needles, I'm now
ready to do the hand manipulation.

00:01:35.255 --> 00:01:39.664
These are fun little cables that add
a bit of texture to your knitting.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.080
Both the knit and the
purl side is interesting.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.669
Making this all over pattern, a
good choice for blankets and scarfs.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:53.170
I'm releasing the first
stitch down three rows.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.389
There will be three strands to relatch.

00:01:58.019 --> 00:02:02.880
I'll latch 2 of the strands and then drop
the latch tool, keeping the stitch in the

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:03.330
hook.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.170
Using a second lanch tool.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.360
I'll drop the next stitch down.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:14.170
Again.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:20.440
There's going to be three strands to latch
and a latch up all three and place this

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.090
stitch on the right hand needle.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.910
This is the first half of our mini cable.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:38.640
Now pick up the first latch tool again,
latch that last strand and hang the stitch

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.110
on the left hand needle.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:47.970
You've reformed two stitches over three
rows and you created a cable on the second

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.820
row without having to count any stitches.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:51.690
Okay.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:58.070
Another quick way of adding texture to
your knitting is to reform columns of

00:02:58.070 --> 00:02:58.820
stitches.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:04.970
You can create ribbing this way, but
sometimes a bit of texture on a reverse

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:08.240
stockinette background is just
what you want for your garment.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:14.870
Traditionally, we knit a section of our
garment and drop a stitch reforming every

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.630
row or every other row for texture.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:22.619
I've often noticed that my stitches
are uneven and look sloppy.

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:26.880
Try this.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:32.310
Knit your piece with a needle out of
work then reform the column of strands.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.929
It saves a bit of time because
you don't have to drop the stitch.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.709
The resulting re-latched
stitches are tighter.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:45.420
And keep in mind that this will impact
your garment stitch gauge a bit.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:47.519
Here.

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:49.200
You can see both methods.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.649
On the left is the reformed drop stitch.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.600
After blocking this woolyarn, the
sloppiness is gone and the stitch sits up

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.000
really nice and high against
that purl background.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.630
Giving great texture on the right is
the reformed needle out of work stitch.

00:04:07.899 --> 00:04:13.540
It lays flatter against the purl side
and it is slightly faster to work.

00:04:15.614 --> 00:04:20.865
Best tip for faster hand manipulation
is having extra tools available.

00:04:21.495 --> 00:04:23.715
This saves you a ton of time.

00:04:24.284 --> 00:04:28.185
You notice that I use two latch
tools when working the mini cables.

00:04:30.094 --> 00:04:35.705
There are multi-pronged transfer tools,
short garter bar sections, adjustable

00:04:35.705 --> 00:04:36.935
transfer tools.

00:04:37.145 --> 00:04:41.705
All of these things come in handy
when working manipulated stitches.

00:04:43.545 --> 00:04:45.525
Thank you for watching this tutorial.

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:50.295
The complete set of cheat sheets
for all machine gauges is available.

